<DocsLayout title="Overview" subtitle="CADCAMFUN brings drawing, solid modelling, toolpaths, project history, and an AI assistant into a local desktop application." description="CADCAMFUN brings drawing, solid modelling, toolpaths, project history, and an AI assistant into a local desktop application." toc={toc}>
  <section id="product" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Product</h2>
    <p>The desktop combines a SolidJS workbench with a Tauri shell. The workbench edits drawings and models, displays geometry in a Three.js viewport, manages machining operations, and previews or exports G-code. Project data, tools, materials, machine configurations, and assistant state are managed by a Bun sidecar and stored locally.</p>
    <p>The computational kernel is shared between the desktop and sidecar. Interactive geometry can run in the webview without an HTTP round trip; persistent operations, provider calls, project history, and workflow execution go through sidecar services. Local storage does not require a hosted application account. AI providers, GitHub operations, payments, and software updates use their configured network services.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="workflow" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Design to machining</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Create or open a project and prepare its drawings, entities, or solid scene. The <a href="/docs/cad">CAD reference</a> covers sketches, B-rep geometry, tessellation, and exchange formats.</li>
      <li>Choose an operation, tool, material, and machine configuration. Generate toolpaths with the <a href="/docs/cam">CAM services</a> and inspect the result in the viewport.</li>
      <li>Use <a href="/docs/setups">machining setups</a> and program documents to retain operation order, work frames, and program settings. Select a registered post for the intended controller.</li>
      <li>Review the generated program and verification results before export. The geometry kernel, post registry, advanced G-code transformations, and machine configuration are distinct parts of this process.</li>
      <li>Record a project snapshot with <a href="/docs/history">history</a>, or run a registered <a href="/docs/workflows">workflow</a> for repeated validation and processing.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The <a href="/docs/ai">assistant</a> invokes application actions through the same service adapters used by the app. The <a href="/docs/mobile">mobile companion</a> connects to a paired desktop; it does not run a second CAD/CAM backend on the phone.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="layout" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Runtime components</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><code>apps/desktop</code></dt><dd>The SolidJS workbench, route adapters, feature modules, viewport, and native Tauri shell.</dd>
      <dt><code>apps/sidecar</code></dt><dd>Authenticated HTTP handlers, repositories, Effect services, local identity, AI integration, companion gateway, and database lifecycle.</dd>
      <dt><code>apps/mobile</code></dt><dd>Expo companion with native pairing transport, project and history views, and assistant access.</dd>
      <dt><code>packages/web</code></dt><dd>This Astro documentation site, Markdown export, documentation assistant, generated guide pages, and local Studio.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>The <a href="/docs/packages">workspace reference</a> describes every library, its public entry point, and its consumers. The <a href="/docs/source-map">source map</a> points to implementation files for common changes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="commands" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Start development</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Repository root">{`bun install
bun run desktop:dev

# Documentation and local Studio
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web dev`}</DocCode>
    <p>The desktop command compiles the sidecar and copies migration resources before starting Tauri. Rust and the platform webview build prerequisites must be installed. To run the documentation server without starting the local Studio API, use <code>bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web start</code>.</p>
    <p>Follow <a href="/docs/guides/quickstart">Quickstart</a> for prerequisites and first launch, or <a href="/docs/guides/local-dev">Local development</a> for isolated sidecar and client loops.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Contracts and persistence</h2>
    <p><code>packages/api/src/index.ts</code> composes the schema-first HTTP contract. The sidecar's <code>ServedApi</code> selects the groups it serves and publishes an OpenAPI document at <code>/api/openapi.json</code>. The shell discovers the dynamically allocated loopback port from a readiness event and gives the webview an endpoint and bearer token.</p>
    <p>The sidecar database is <code>cadcamfun.db</code> under its configured data directory. The database layer enables foreign keys and WAL, applies configured migrations, and backs up an existing file before a pending upgrade. The documentation Studio has its own database path selection; it does not discover the running sidecar's file automatically.</p>
    <p>These pages describe the implementation in this repository. A package export, an HTTP endpoint, a desktop control, and a released installer are different integration surfaces; consult the relevant reference for supported behavior and limits.</p>
  </section>


  <section id="choose-a-path" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Choose a working path</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Start here</th><th>What to retain</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Edit a part</td><td><a href="/docs/cad">CAD geometry</a> and <a href="/docs/viewport">viewport controls</a></td><td>The editable drawing or feature history, including units and placement.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Generate machining output</td><td><a href="/docs/cam">CAM</a> and <a href="/docs/setups">saved programs</a></td><td>The captured geometry, stock, tools, machine settings, emitted NC, and applicable verification evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Review a change</td><td><a href="/docs/history">Project history</a></td><td>The commit and structural diff, plus the current review verdicts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automate a repeated check</td><td><a href="/docs/workflows">Workflows</a></td><td>The committed workflow file, selected ref, registered steps, and individual check results.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Build an integration</td><td><a href="/docs/api">API reference</a></td><td>The correct token type, exact endpoint contract, and result/error handling.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Project saves, resource versions, whole-project commits, remote synchronization, and generated guide artifacts are separate operations. Saving a toolpath does not automatically commit it, and a successful GitHub push does not verify its machining output.</p>
    <p>For a first API integration, follow the <a href="/docs/guides/api-tour">request walkthrough</a>. It shows the public health probe, protected calls, project creation, and preference storage with captured handler responses.</p>
  </section>


  <section id="operations" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Operational documentation</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/docs/guides">guide library</a> walks through complete tasks. Start with <a href="/docs/guides/project-lifecycle">project creation and saved work</a>, then use <a href="/docs/guides/native-manufacturing">native manufacturing</a> to take a captured design through generation and program persistence. Both explain what is saved and what remains derived or unsaved state.</p>
    <p>For integration work, the <a href="/docs/api">API resource reference</a> covers every declared operation with request and response schemas. The <a href="/docs/guides/api-clients">client integration guide</a> covers Promise and Effect construction, authentication, cancellation, and error handling. The <a href="/docs/guides/ai-streaming">streaming guide</a> adds conversation recovery and interactive permission/question handling.</p>
    <p>For operation and support, consult <a href="/docs/configuration">runtime configuration</a>, <a href="/docs/guides/desktop-troubleshooting">desktop diagnosis</a>, and <a href="/docs/guides/companion-pairing">companion pairing</a>. Each keeps the native shell, sidecar, database, provider, phone gateway, and documentation deployment distinct so a failure can be investigated at its actual boundary.</p>
    <p>For extensions and teamwork, use <a href="/docs/guides/plugin-authoring">plugin authoring</a> and <a href="/docs/guides/project-history">branches, reviews, and workflows</a>. These guides document supported module exports, current host behavior, structural merge rules, committed workflow inputs, and service-backed side effects.</p>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
